#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86baff91-0829-5b81-9da3-f645cfba8a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346773} {:answer/id #uuid "a55302ac-1828-5914-9048-101364834b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346774} {:answer/id #uuid "b2107981-70bb-5b68-9161-64fd41deef1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346775} {:answer/id #uuid "98ca66fa-13d2-5cfb-a7dc-745f97c1bb22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346776} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa7bbde-545f-59fe-a046-164063e56074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aa7bbde-545f-59fe-a046-164063e56074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346777} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca7d0ce-68bd-5fe8-9eda-cf6645243508" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f87a306-8845-5bbe-b708-b948d9b92fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-156182" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{4}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cac3ee0-abf1-511b-8656-df6c4de0e4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346780} {:answer/id #uuid "882e2bc8-40aa-59b2-a10d-6237dabc4fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346781} {:answer/id #uuid "fea5b75e-613e-511b-b77e-d7f476afaf9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346782} {:answer/id #uuid "d10889e2-126c-56ff-af17-666e924e98c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346783} {:answer/id #uuid "3aeafbdb-cadd-51ca-a1c8-4115a25a497a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aeafbdb-cadd-51ca-a1c8-4115a25a497a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346784} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b837d37-8880-5d2d-aecf-4b5c76d0b171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83ef394f-2bed-56a9-96fd-7d12a1febb1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-156183" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({2}^{(-x)}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{(-x)}}) \\\\  & =({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{(1/2)^{x}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}-{2}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8dcc751-8cfc-5b4f-ae52-262b527d6cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8dcc751-8cfc-5b4f-ae52-262b527d6cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293979} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca32e62c-fcb6-51a7-bc44-8fcea252735a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a28493e5-35c4-5848-af51-41583c3d2bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334359" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[5]{(-x)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4ad931-d6c5-5a3d-914c-c276a009d84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156611} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaf6f33-be77-5b1a-8c72-98ed6a1a8fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156612} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc0cd67-7df9-568a-bc9b-0c7d65892443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156613} {:answer/id #uuid "e35981b8-f69e-5e88-946e-767277ca3b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156614} {:answer/id #uuid "17d4dea2-d5ec-5f4c-8bfc-1c46ee872135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d4dea2-d5ec-5f4c-8bfc-1c46ee872135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156615} :answer-field/id #uuid "799ba412-3bb6-5597-813b-29c7be335d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e1f8f9-d432-5676-a7be-df1e563ec326" :question/math-academy-id "q-88209" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17220ac1-9331-5b65-9cb9-280c772288bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293982} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8f2890-f74d-59ee-94bd-a52d5ca0ebf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293983} {:answer/id #uuid "9f635f75-c4fb-5c76-b9ed-2e6e1c02f6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293984} {:answer/id #uuid "eeab11c1-1365-5363-961e-7eb815d63140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293985} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaf3502-3c8c-5971-84f1-5fb372470c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeab11c1-1365-5363-961e-7eb815d63140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293985} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2328d7f-6c9b-5526-af03-aca8958dcf1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72674b4f-6b99-5086-b3af-6306160b6663" :question/math-academy-id "q-88692" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
